  "result": "I've implemented `slugify` in `src/strings.js` and added 11 tests for it in `test/strings.test.js`; `npm test` passes all 13 tests, including the two existing ones.\n\n**How it works:**\n1. It lowercases the text, then splits accented letters into a base letter plus accent marks and drops the marks. That turns `Tiếng Việt` into `tieng viet` and `Crème Brûlée` into `creme brulee`.\n2. Some Latin letters have no separate accent to drop, so a small lookup table replaces them: `đ`→`d`, `ð`→`d`, `ł`→`l`, `ø`→`o`, `ß`→`ss`, `æ`→`ae`, `œ`→`oe`, `þ`→`th`. `Đ` is covered because lowercasing happens first.\n3. Every run of characters outside `a-z0-9` becomes one `-`, and hyphens at the start and end are removed.\n4. The result is cut to 50 characters, and any hyphens left at the end of the cut are removed.\n\n**Tests** cover: lowercasing, Vietnamese text, `đ`/`Đ`, other accented Latin letters, collapsing runs of separators, trimming, digits, empty and symbols-only input, the 50-character limit, a cut that would otherwise end in a hyphen, and an input of exactly 50 characters that is kept whole.\n\nText in non-Latin scripts, such as Chinese or Cyrillic, has no ASCII form under these rules, so it is removed. A title written only in those scripts gives an empty slug.",
